00:01:51.371 --> 00:01:53.460
Something that we're
gonna be thinking about,

00:01:53.461 --> 00:01:57.250
puzzling about, always coming
at from different directions,

00:01:57.251 --> 00:01:58.510
probably for generations.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.250
That's the scale of problem
I think we're dealing with

00:02:02.251 --> 00:02:05.670
in relation to knowledge and
freedom.

00:02:05.671 --> 00:02:10.320
And to get started, I just
want to go back, particularly

00:02:10.321 --> 00:02:14.060
maybe you heard my white board
talk about six weeks ago or

00:02:14.061 --> 00:02:16.940
you didn't, but anyway, to give
you the starting point of how I

00:02:16.941 --> 00:02:20.390
approach social theory at all.

00:02:20.391 --> 00:02:24.760
So my broad interest is
the relations between media

00:02:24.761 --> 00:02:28.770
in a very broad sense, including
information, data collection,

00:02:28.771 --> 00:02:32.340
everything we now compact
into that word media, and

00:02:32.341 --> 00:02:33.980
communication generally.

00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:38.829
And something we might
still want to call society,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.078
the social.

00:02:40.079 --> 00:02:43.260
I put it in scare quotes because
I'm not assuming a neatly

00:02:43.261 --> 00:02:46.047
functioning order within
national boundaries,

00:02:46.048 --> 00:02:47.250
that's absurd now.

00:02:47.251 --> 00:02:48.450
We're not talking about that.

00:02:48.451 --> 00:02:49.780
But we are talking about

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:56.030
processes of ordering
something like the social, so

00:02:56.031 --> 00:02:59.470
it feels social,
which is called the social.

00:02:59.471 --> 00:03:02.950
And we're talking about
the role in that ordering

00:03:02.951 --> 00:03:06.005
of media infrastructures in
the broadest possible sense.

00:03:06.006 --> 00:03:09.060
And I think that's a issue for
social theory, which is

00:03:09.061 --> 00:03:12.720
also an issue for economic
theory, political theory.

00:03:12.721 --> 00:03:16.870
We all assume some minimal level
order without which we wouldn't

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:20.970
be able to talk about any of
the things we want to talk about

00:03:20.971 --> 00:03:22.025
in doing social science.

00:03:22.026 --> 00:03:26.200
And I'm particularly interested
in the consequences for

00:03:26.201 --> 00:03:30.354
social order in this broad
sense of the fundamental but

00:03:30.355 --> 00:03:34.630
completely banal fact today
which is absolutely astonishing.

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:38.278
Which is the embedding
of computing power and

00:03:38.279 --> 00:03:43.270
therefore two way or multi-way
connection that's possible

00:03:43.271 --> 00:03:47.502
through computing power,
at every point in space.

00:03:47.503 --> 00:03:51.155
And because information is
archived at every point in

00:03:51.156 --> 00:03:55.295
space time, creating utterly
new types of feedback loops,

00:03:55.296 --> 00:03:58.900
which we have not even
begun to theorize.

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:01.180
That's the problem
I'm interested in.

00:04:01.181 --> 00:04:05.420
And at the very least, that sort
of deep embedding of computer

00:04:05.421 --> 00:04:08.840
power, parallelizations if you
like, creates new forms of

00:04:08.841 --> 00:04:13.200
dependence on computing systems,
obviously, which are the basis

00:04:13.201 --> 00:04:16.910
of business models, new forms
of social interdependence.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.940
And, since no interdependence
comes without tensions,

00:04:22.941 --> 00:04:26.150
social tensions, which we need
to seriously think about.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.670
And if you like, also,
a new type of social complexity.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.830
How can we think about this
multi-dimensional order that

00:04:36.831 --> 00:04:40.250
has to be arising somehow in
the middle of all this change?

00:04:40.251 --> 00:04:42.480
That's the thing
I'm interested in.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:46.124
And I just want to introduce
from my previous book,

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:49.007
The Mediated Construction
of Reality,

00:04:49.008 --> 00:04:51.565
one concept that I
think is useful.

00:04:51.566 --> 00:04:53.543
I won't refer back to
it in detail later on,

00:04:53.544 --> 00:04:56.161
but it will give you a flavor of
how I think about this sort of

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:57.150
complexity.

00:04:57.151 --> 00:05:00.530
And I talked about it briefly
in the white board talk and

00:05:00.531 --> 00:05:05.910
that's a concept from
the German theorist

00:05:05.911 --> 00:05:09.641
often neglected now in Britain
and the states, Norbert Elias.

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.360
He was a refugee from
Nazi Germany and

00:05:12.361 --> 00:05:14.540
lived a lot of his
life in Britain.

00:05:14.541 --> 00:05:18.110
He developed a concept to social
complexity which he called

00:05:18.111 --> 00:05:20.030
figurations.

00:05:20.031 --> 00:05:23.710
And figurations is interesting
because it's very different from

00:05:23.711 --> 00:05:26.890
the concept we tend to rely on
to understand complexity today,

00:05:26.891 --> 00:05:28.460
which is networks.

00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:30.270
Everyone understands a network.

00:05:30.271 --> 00:05:34.400
Sets of nodes, physical links or
informational links, which

00:05:34.401 --> 00:05:38.730
create a more complex network
space, maybe extremely complex.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.098
Now Elias was also
interested in networks, but

00:05:43.099 --> 00:05:47.309
he added a crucial ingredient
which is that they were dynamic,

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.179
that they were lattice
works of tensions.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.938
In other words, at each point
in space is an entity that's

00:05:53.939 --> 00:05:58.017
pulling the entity at the other
end of that line in the network.

00:05:58.018 --> 00:06:00.942
They're dynamic and they're
dynamic because there's meaning

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:04.330
and content, there's something
at stake in the network.

00:06:04.331 --> 00:06:07.110
That creates a totally different
type of dynamic tension

00:06:07.111 --> 00:06:11.400
from the one we can theorize
the notion of networks.

00:06:11.401 --> 00:06:14.050
Which is apparently our
most advanced concept for

00:06:14.051 --> 00:06:14.730
complexity but

00:06:14.731 --> 00:06:18.235
I think barely even gets going
in thinking about complexity.

00:06:18.236 --> 00:06:22.691
He said the behavior of so many
separate people intermeshes,

00:06:22.692 --> 00:06:26.287
it's like a fence,
a wire fence pulling to create

00:06:26.288 --> 00:06:30.415
the strength of the fence to
form into woven structures.

00:06:30.416 --> 00:06:32.180
I just want to illustrate
that in a diagram.

00:06:32.181 --> 00:06:34.810
So here's from our book,

00:06:34.811 --> 00:06:36.620
you can see that's much
more than a network,

00:06:36.621 --> 00:06:40.960
of course, because each entity
is pulling on the other.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.880
It's not just linked,
it's pulling.

00:06:44.881 --> 00:06:48.150
And of course each
person in the grouping,

00:06:48.151 --> 00:06:50.130
it could be a game of cards,
a game of football,

00:06:50.131 --> 00:06:53.600
it could be a corporation,
is being themselves pulled in

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:56.170
multiple directions by
other spaces in society.

00:06:56.171 --> 00:06:59.495
So in other words, already,
even in this simple concept, and

00:06:59.496 --> 00:07:03.810
that's our version of Elias'
diagram from the early 1970s.

00:07:03.811 --> 00:07:07.030
That's how advanced he was in
his thinking about complexity.

00:07:07.031 --> 00:07:10.062
Already that implies
a multidimensional order.

00:07:10.063 --> 00:07:12.500
A tention in higher dimensions,

00:07:12.501 --> 00:07:15.730
which results from this
apparently one dimensional,

00:07:15.731 --> 00:07:18.370
two dimensional plane,
which is interesting.

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:19.860
Just to go back a second.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:25.110
The question then is taking his
concept from the late 70s and

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.630
actually he already had
the ideas in the early 30s,

00:07:27.631 --> 00:07:29.920
which makes it even
more astonishing.

00:07:29.921 --> 00:07:33.920
How can we think about social
order in higher dimensions,

00:07:33.921 --> 00:07:35.160
really higher dimensions,

00:07:35.161 --> 00:07:38.450
when we take account of all
the institutions in society?

00:07:38.451 --> 00:07:41.930
What we call in that previous
book figurations of figurations.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.080
Well here's a diagram of
how that would look like.

00:07:46.081 --> 00:07:47.692
So you have the initial
figuration,

00:07:47.693 --> 00:07:49.616
which already implies
higher dimensions.

00:07:49.617 --> 00:07:52.817
But it itself,
the overall push or tension or

00:07:52.818 --> 00:07:56.524
weight if you like at that
figuration is itself being

00:07:56.525 --> 00:08:00.010
pulled by other
institutional forces.

00:08:00.011 --> 00:08:02.786
So if we think about
Facebook as an institution,

00:08:02.787 --> 00:08:04.990
the figuration of figurations.

00:08:04.991 --> 00:08:07.535
The group of people who
are designing policy in Facebook

00:08:07.536 --> 00:08:09.120
are human beings
like the rest of us,

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.250
doing the best to solve
whatever they have to solve and

00:08:12.251 --> 00:08:14.200
they have their own
personal histories.

00:08:14.201 --> 00:08:18.021
But they're, of course, being
pulled by the economic forces,

00:08:18.022 --> 00:08:21.423
which are the basis of the
business model, which in turn,

00:08:21.424 --> 00:08:22.471
is being pulled.

00:08:22.472 --> 00:08:25.962
By the brand and all the social
tensions that it creates to

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:28.630
represent Facebook
in certain ways.

00:08:28.631 --> 00:08:32.240
Which is turn is being pulled
by government, law and so

00:08:32.241 --> 00:08:36.530
on, not at infinitum but
on many, many dimensions.

00:08:36.531 --> 00:08:39.560
We're looking at hundreds of
dimensions, pooling together,

00:08:39.561 --> 00:08:43.658
creating an extremely
complex type of order.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:49.295
So Let's take, put that

00:08:49.296 --> 00:08:54.000
in the background, and now think
about two particular problems.

00:08:54.001 --> 00:08:55.040
But before I get
onto my problems,

00:08:55.041 --> 00:08:57.620
there's just one more
bit of introduction.

00:08:57.621 --> 00:09:00.910
We like to think, of course,
particularly in media studies,

00:09:00.911 --> 00:09:04.220
which sometimes presents itself
as study, what's right now?

00:09:04.221 --> 00:09:05.539
What's absolutely new?

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:09.690
We like to think that no one
has ever thought about these

00:09:09.691 --> 00:09:13.330
problems before, but,
of course, that's not true.

00:09:13.331 --> 00:09:16.400
The whole history of social
theory has been puzzling in

00:09:16.401 --> 00:09:20.740
different moments of trying to
what the hell is going on here?

00:09:20.741 --> 00:09:22.410
We don't have the concepts for
this,

00:09:22.411 --> 00:09:25.730
this is what generated
sociology in the first place.

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:29.000
It's what generated the crisis
in sociology in the 60s, and

00:09:29.001 --> 00:09:30.570
so on and so forth.

00:09:30.571 --> 00:09:33.610
So, can we find any
parallels for the sorts of

00:09:33.611 --> 00:09:36.390
radical transformations
we're seeing today?

00:09:36.391 --> 00:09:37.890
Well we can.

00:09:37.891 --> 00:09:40.280
It's a great book from
the 80s James Beniger,

00:09:40.281 --> 00:09:44.310
The Control Revolution which you
may not know if communication is

00:09:44.311 --> 00:09:48.530
strongly recommended because
it's one of the very few books

00:09:48.531 --> 00:09:52.510
that starts out form
the basis that communication

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:56.220
that's the little area we
look at through the lens and

00:09:56.221 --> 00:09:59.440
media studies is actually
the site where all sort of other

00:09:59.441 --> 00:10:00.770
forces are being pulled.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:05.170
They happen to generate things
like the telegraph system,

00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:06.540
newspapers.

00:10:06.541 --> 00:10:09.820
But that's because of all
these intercepting forces

00:10:09.821 --> 00:10:12.600
that operate through them,
that make them necessary,

00:10:12.601 --> 00:10:15.540
that sustain them, that
generate crisis through them.

00:10:15.541 --> 00:10:19.650
So he sees communications at
the site of an intersection of

00:10:19.651 --> 00:10:21.420
massive rep social.

00:10:21.421 --> 00:10:24.900
And economic change crossing
areas such as transport,

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.600
spacial organization, business.

00:10:28.601 --> 00:10:30.490
To give you the simple
example from his book,

00:10:30.491 --> 00:10:32.920
which everyone quotes
because it's so vivid.

00:10:32.921 --> 00:10:38.050
He explained that at a certain
point it was important to

00:10:38.051 --> 00:10:42.880
send the news down the railway
line that the train was coming.

00:10:42.881 --> 00:10:45.060
Because there only was one line.

00:10:45.061 --> 00:10:46.540
And what if a train was
coming the other way?

00:10:46.541 --> 00:10:50.970
There are many accidents
when the ability to send

00:10:50.971 --> 00:10:54.250
the message down the line and
to do it in an ordered precise

00:10:54.251 --> 00:10:59.940
way that was effective had not
yet been planned and developed.

00:10:59.941 --> 00:11:03.440
That's a very simple way
of how new transport

00:11:03.441 --> 00:11:08.180
possibilities require
absolutely new communication

00:11:08.181 --> 00:11:12.350
in effective structures which
itself links into economics,

00:11:12.351 --> 00:11:13.660
business, and so on.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:18.340
So that's interesting.

00:11:18.341 --> 00:11:23.450
So can we think about
today's problems is also

00:11:23.451 --> 00:11:26.310
what Beniger called
problems of control.

00:11:26.311 --> 00:11:28.060
And just to throw in
one other example,

00:11:28.061 --> 00:11:31.320
because it's an illustration
of sort of great conversations

00:11:31.321 --> 00:11:33.460
I'm having here in Microsoft.

00:11:33.461 --> 00:11:36.360
Tomorrow in our reading group
on radical social theory

00:11:36.361 --> 00:11:39.130
we're discussing another great
historical parallel which will

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:44.310
be work, he has the great
transformation which

00:11:44.311 --> 00:11:48.820
was on the emergence of markets
in the early 19th century,

00:11:48.821 --> 00:11:50.530
in the industrial capitalism and

00:11:50.531 --> 00:11:53.490
how that didn't just happen
because markets were somehow

00:11:53.491 --> 00:11:57.239
natural and just waiting to
be pulled out of the box.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.770
The sorts of assumptions that
make a market possible, if I do

00:12:02.771 --> 00:12:06.660
that, you, of course, are gonna
do that in return have to be

00:12:06.661 --> 00:12:11.505
socially constructed, they have
to be built and much of that

00:12:11.506 --> 00:12:14.505
building was very painful and
very difficult because it went

00:12:14.506 --> 00:12:19.175
against previous assumptions
the way the economy worked.

00:12:19.176 --> 00:12:21.555
So, another example.

00:12:21.556 --> 00:12:25.235
But taking those two together,
both of them bring out for

00:12:25.236 --> 00:12:28.995
me that even if your
interest is economics or

00:12:28.996 --> 00:12:33.110
let's say government how
the socialist constructed.

00:12:33.111 --> 00:12:35.760
How it comes to be
socially possible

00:12:35.761 --> 00:12:38.520
that human entities could
be doing other things,

00:12:38.521 --> 00:12:41.335
interact that way is
enormously important.

00:12:41.336 --> 00:12:44.690
So I will want to argue that
social theory, whether it's done

00:12:44.691 --> 00:12:48.450
well or badly is potentially
very important contribution to

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:53.020
economic theory, political
theory and government and so.

00:12:53.021 --> 00:12:54.660
So that's what I'm
gonna think about.

00:12:54.661 --> 00:12:56.070
So I'm gonna move on now to the,

00:12:56.071 --> 00:13:00.120
what they're calling the
knowledge problem in the age of

00:13:00.121 --> 00:13:05.135
big data I don't need to say
anymore about what big data is.

00:13:06.425 --> 00:13:08.555
It's in the popular discourse,
and

00:13:08.556 --> 00:13:11.495
many of us in this room
are experts in what it means in

00:13:11.496 --> 00:13:16.495
terms of the practices of
collecting and processing data.

00:13:16.496 --> 00:13:17.355
And beyond that,

00:13:17.356 --> 00:13:20.555
the stories we're telling
ourselves about the new types of

00:13:20.556 --> 00:13:23.435
knowledge we're getting of
the social through big data.

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:27.490
There's a lot of myth making
about what big data is

00:13:27.491 --> 00:13:28.435
doing to the social.

00:13:28.436 --> 00:13:31.220
Well there's a lot of serious
thinking about what big data

00:13:31.221 --> 00:13:33.850
could do to genuinely
transform our knowledge of

00:13:33.851 --> 00:13:35.670
the social world.

00:13:35.671 --> 00:13:37.800
All that on top of
the practices themselves.

00:13:37.801 --> 00:13:40.240
So let's think
about this problem.

00:13:40.241 --> 00:13:41.520
What do I mean by the social?

00:13:41.521 --> 00:13:44.440
I'm always asked this so I get
this on the table right away.

00:13:44.441 --> 00:13:47.140
The social is not something I
can not neatly define because it

00:13:47.141 --> 00:13:48.250
can not be neatly defined.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:54.030
But it's the word we use to
point at that massive domain of

00:13:54.031 --> 00:13:59.640
human activity which is based
in interactions and connections,

00:13:59.641 --> 00:14:02.859
without which we can't have
anything like human life.

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:07.350
We can't have a few people
existing in some way for

00:14:07.351 --> 00:14:10.860
a few hours or
months in the desert and

00:14:10.861 --> 00:14:12.700
dying, utterly
wretched nonsense.

00:14:12.701 --> 00:14:15.800
That's why anything recognizable
as successful human life

00:14:15.801 --> 00:14:17.960
requires interaction,
connection.

00:14:17.961 --> 00:14:20.640
And that's what we're pointing
to when you use the word social.

00:14:20.641 --> 00:14:22.980
There is no better definition,
then.

00:14:22.981 --> 00:14:27.240
I got that from William Sewell,
the Chicago social theorist.

00:14:27.241 --> 00:14:30.020
But that's just the start of
a few interesting questions when

00:14:30.021 --> 00:14:31.990
we think about this
knowledge problem.

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:36.200
Because social knowledge isn't
just our knowledge of each other

00:14:36.201 --> 00:14:37.030
as individuals.

00:14:37.031 --> 00:14:38.700
That's essential but

00:14:38.701 --> 00:14:42.290
it's the stories we tell
about the social overall.

00:14:42.291 --> 00:14:45.420
Because they're part of how
we situate each of our lives.

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:48.490
So we already get into
a higher order problem,

00:14:48.491 --> 00:14:50.690
what is social knowledge.

00:14:50.691 --> 00:14:52.060
There's a metaproblem,
in other words,

00:14:52.061 --> 00:14:54.860
of how the social world
knows itself at all.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:56.870
But not just in it.

00:14:56.871 --> 00:14:59.390
We have accounts of it.

00:14:59.391 --> 00:15:01.460
On the basis which we
say you can't do that.

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.970
That's unacceptable or
things can't work that way.

00:15:05.971 --> 00:15:08.730
I have a right therefore
to challenge that.

00:15:08.731 --> 00:15:12.540
So, social knowledge exists
in various dimensions and

00:15:12.541 --> 00:15:15.190
there's always
a neglected level as well,

00:15:15.191 --> 00:15:18.350
which Pierre Bourdieu was one of
the first people to point out.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:24.800
The book in the 70s
we forget that

00:15:24.801 --> 00:15:26.570
social science
itself is knowledge.

00:15:26.571 --> 00:15:29.130
Like all knowledge
has to be produced.

00:15:29.131 --> 00:15:31.710
Humans have to be in
institutions very often.

00:15:31.711 --> 00:15:33.380
They have to travel
to the right place.

00:15:33.381 --> 00:15:35.130
They need tools and so on.

00:15:35.131 --> 00:15:39.690
There's always the hidden
social conditions of producing

00:15:39.691 --> 00:15:42.720
what we know about
the social and

00:15:42.721 --> 00:15:45.250
we need a sociology to
understand that too.

00:15:45.251 --> 00:15:48.670
So there's many dimensions to
how we come to know the social

00:15:48.671 --> 00:15:51.490
at all, and those dimensions
are now changing.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:56.302
And there's a tendency,
always which

00:15:57.384 --> 00:16:00.700
pointed out to ignore those
underlying conditions,

00:16:00.701 --> 00:16:03.770
which say what can be
knowledge of the social.

00:16:03.771 --> 00:16:06.420
We assume we just know,
we just see right in there.

00:16:06.421 --> 00:16:09.780
But that possibility seeing
right into things is it so

00:16:09.781 --> 00:16:13.040
socially produced into
various forms of expertise,

00:16:13.041 --> 00:16:15.620
privilege, institutional
resource.

00:16:15.621 --> 00:16:18.360
And so on, which are really
important, especially

00:16:18.361 --> 00:16:21.880
when an ability to see things
is changing, as it is right now.

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:26.800
And we can also think about
how the knowledge of those

00:16:26.801 --> 00:16:30.650
conditions under which social
knowledge is produced,

00:16:30.651 --> 00:16:34.620
is it's deliberately
made opaque, very often.

00:16:34.621 --> 00:16:38.825
We don't always want people
to see into the engine room,

00:16:38.826 --> 00:16:41.458
to see how we claim
to see the world.

00:16:41.459 --> 00:16:44.109
Cuz that would
lead to challenge.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.503
And I think we're in a stage
where the certain things

00:16:46.504 --> 00:16:48.800
are very opaque about
the new types of knowledge.

00:16:48.801 --> 00:16:50.050
So I wanted to come on to that.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:53.928
Now, this is where I think
historical comparison

00:16:53.929 --> 00:16:55.696
becomes really useful because,

00:16:55.697 --> 00:16:58.611
I've been working on this
a lot while I've been here.

00:16:58.612 --> 00:17:01.174
Because if we just started
from what we have now,

00:17:01.175 --> 00:17:04.052
it's bewildering very fast
changing and therefore,

00:17:04.053 --> 00:17:07.052
the natural thing is to say,
well let's go with those who

00:17:07.053 --> 00:17:09.510
are doing the work, and
follow what they say.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.770
But then we have no
historical comparison at all.

00:17:13.771 --> 00:17:16.520
We have no sense of whether

00:17:16.521 --> 00:17:19.735
previous societies have gone
through similar things.

00:17:19.736 --> 00:17:23.190
And if they have, did it work
out radically different way?

00:17:23.191 --> 00:17:24.755
And that's why I want
to suggest to you for

00:17:24.756 --> 00:17:27.770
a few minutes that
the comparison with the 19th

00:17:27.771 --> 00:17:30.800
century to early 20th century
leaves us some very surprising

00:17:30.801 --> 00:17:33.440
differences from
where we are today.

00:17:36.213 --> 00:17:38.640
I won't go into the detail
of the history statistics,

00:17:38.641 --> 00:17:39.748
it's not my expertise,

00:17:39.749 --> 00:17:42.510
but I'm rereading a lot of the
books by those who are experts.

00:17:42.511 --> 00:17:45.620
And it throws up some very
interesting differences.

00:17:45.621 --> 00:17:48.200
So in the early 19th century,
late 18th century,

00:17:48.201 --> 00:17:50.870
there was a radical shift in
the knowledge of the social,

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.070
particularly in Europe and
North America.

00:17:54.071 --> 00:17:58.101
So the world shifted from one
in which there was human nature,

00:17:58.102 --> 00:18:02.141
which was largely fixed, and
written about from Aristotle.

00:18:02.142 --> 00:18:05.630
And there was human variation.

00:18:05.631 --> 00:18:08.220
Individuals were of
course slightly variable.

00:18:08.221 --> 00:18:10.070
But we didn't know much
about that and therefore,

00:18:10.071 --> 00:18:12.260
it wasn't very interesting and
nothing ordered or

00:18:12.261 --> 00:18:16.010
structured could be said about
it, that was all there was.

00:18:16.011 --> 00:18:18.680
So there wasn't really much of
a development of social science.

00:18:19.837 --> 00:18:24.189
Statistics based on counting,
gathering data counting starting

00:18:24.190 --> 00:18:27.453
to reserve numerical and
statistical patterns,

00:18:27.454 --> 00:18:31.038
radically change what could
be said about the social.

00:18:31.039 --> 00:18:33.999
And many would argue actually
created the possibility of

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.081
a social, of the idea there was
something ordered out there

00:18:37.082 --> 00:18:38.265
which was the social.

00:18:40.708 --> 00:18:43.651
And this was
a complex social and

00:18:43.652 --> 00:18:47.148
of course highly
political process.

00:18:47.149 --> 00:18:51.017
As Ted Porter, one of the
leading historians of numbers

00:18:51.018 --> 00:18:53.653
statistics writes,
the credibility

00:18:53.654 --> 00:18:58.367
of numbers doesn't just exist,
it's a social and moral problem.

00:19:00.217 --> 00:19:03.060
People fight over which
numbers are better.

00:19:03.061 --> 00:19:05.990
Who has the right to
generate numbers and

00:19:05.991 --> 00:19:07.080
apply them to the world?

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:11.200
Power must be exercised in
a variety of ways to make

00:19:11.201 --> 00:19:14.160
the measurements and
tallies valid.

00:19:14.161 --> 00:19:16.900
Well, I think it's pretty
obvious we're in a such a moment

00:19:16.901 --> 00:19:17.890
of history right now.

00:19:17.891 --> 00:19:21.124
There's a massive battle going
on about the measurements and

00:19:21.125 --> 00:19:21.877
the tallies.

00:19:22.935 --> 00:19:26.571
And he also says that when
a power game is won and

00:19:26.572 --> 00:19:31.236
certain measurements and
tallies become normative, they

00:19:31.237 --> 00:19:36.660
are very effective in changing
the world in certain ways.

00:19:36.661 --> 00:19:39.481
Or as Ian Hacking puts it
in another great book,

00:19:39.482 --> 00:19:41.038
ways of making up people.

00:19:42.056 --> 00:19:44.991
Making people who can now be
talked about in new ways.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:50.246
And without going into any
details, those of you in

00:19:50.247 --> 00:19:54.953
economics will obviously know
the history was the emergence of

00:19:54.954 --> 00:19:59.393
extraordinary new ideas of
the average, the normal person

00:19:59.394 --> 00:20:03.950
around which statistical
variation always tended to vary.

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:08.164
This was an extraordinary
concept, the statistical norm.

00:20:10.307 --> 00:20:13.070
And that was based in
very material tools,

00:20:13.071 --> 00:20:16.142
putting data together in
packages that could be

00:20:16.143 --> 00:20:18.670
then talked about as one thing.

00:20:18.671 --> 00:20:22.830
The idea of the map which
had data encoded into it.

00:20:22.831 --> 00:20:23.650
A great example,

00:20:23.651 --> 00:20:26.820
because I see if I walk around
the Geography Department at LSC,

00:20:26.821 --> 00:20:29.680
they still have it on the walls.

00:20:29.681 --> 00:20:32.826
It's Charles Booth's Map
of London Poverty,

00:20:32.827 --> 00:20:35.814
based on his discovery
of eight dimensions of

00:20:35.815 --> 00:20:39.127
poverty which came out
of statistical analysis.

00:20:40.310 --> 00:20:43.170
And, it was put up on the wall
in all sorts of institutes.

00:20:43.171 --> 00:20:45.190
So we can think about
how London is organized.

00:20:47.678 --> 00:20:50.897
Some interesting
history by Dan Balcer.

00:20:50.898 --> 00:20:52.892
Historian in the insurance
industry on,

00:20:52.893 --> 00:20:55.742
which very is parallel to what
we're going through today,

00:20:55.743 --> 00:20:58.620
the emergence of new
data aggregates.

00:20:58.621 --> 00:20:59.940
So that's,

00:20:59.941 --> 00:21:04.220
if you like in Snapshoddy, what
Anne Desrosieres, another writer

00:21:04.221 --> 00:21:08.910
about statistics calls the
concrete history of abstraction.

00:21:08.911 --> 00:21:10.960
Abstraction doesn't
just come from nowhere,

00:21:10.961 --> 00:21:15.370
it has to be built in
particular resources.

00:21:15.371 --> 00:21:17.509
It's hard to speak
abstractly and

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:19.285
get it to stick on the world.

00:21:20.962 --> 00:21:22.480
So what are the parallels today?

00:21:22.481 --> 00:21:24.570
Well, let's just sum up what
my argument's gonna be and

00:21:24.571 --> 00:21:26.890
I'll go through some
more detail in a moment.

00:21:26.891 --> 00:21:29.880
I think today's massive
explosion of social knowledge

00:21:29.881 --> 00:21:33.240
for big data has two very
distinctive features compared

00:21:33.241 --> 00:21:34.870
with the 19th century.

00:21:34.871 --> 00:21:36.254
The first is the depth.

00:21:37.435 --> 00:21:39.813
Almost infinite depth and

00:21:39.814 --> 00:21:45.790
the absolute continuity of
which data i's collected.

00:21:45.791 --> 00:21:47.666
This is no parallel
in the 19th century.

00:21:47.667 --> 00:21:51.500
There were censuses
every ten years.

00:21:51.501 --> 00:21:55.355
There were various other tools
to gather data where you could

00:21:55.356 --> 00:21:57.024
put it together somehow.

00:21:57.025 --> 00:21:59.638
There was nothing remotely
similar to the depth and

00:21:59.639 --> 00:22:01.537
continuity we now take for
granted.

00:22:03.182 --> 00:22:07.437
And secondly,
who's gathering the data?

00:22:07.438 --> 00:22:08.900
Who controls the data?

00:22:08.901 --> 00:22:09.649
On what terms?

00:22:12.548 --> 00:22:13.632
As we'll see, it's very

00:22:13.633 --> 00:22:16.060
different today from the way
it was in the 19th century.

00:22:16.061 --> 00:22:18.500
So just going a bit more detail.

00:22:18.501 --> 00:22:21.808
The point about continuity,
as I said,

00:22:21.809 --> 00:22:26.392
data in the 19th century
was gathered episodically.

00:22:26.393 --> 00:22:29.021
Therefore, there was
no n equals all,

00:22:29.022 --> 00:22:31.180
n equals all was unimaginable.

00:22:32.994 --> 00:22:34.927
There was,
n was relatively small and

00:22:34.928 --> 00:22:38.011
might include the whole country
if you could compel people to

00:22:38.012 --> 00:22:40.809
fill in the census, but
that was only every ten years.

00:22:42.457 --> 00:22:46.908
And the gap in between
was not known.

00:22:46.909 --> 00:22:48.991
Insurance companies tried
to do better than that, but

00:22:48.992 --> 00:22:50.540
there were limits to
what they could do.

00:22:52.347 --> 00:22:56.546
The key point through I think is
that social knowledge today is

00:22:56.547 --> 00:23:00.040
increasingly generated
by private capacities.

00:23:00.041 --> 00:23:02.579
I call them capacities cuz
it's not just corporations.

00:23:03.610 --> 00:23:06.788
Any business that starts up
today almost certainly has in

00:23:06.789 --> 00:23:10.500
its business model, creating
a platform with apps linked to

00:23:10.501 --> 00:23:13.420
apps and APIs to gather data.

00:23:13.421 --> 00:23:16.650
Either for it's own purposes or
to sell that data on or

00:23:16.651 --> 00:23:19.710
to somehow make itself
valuable to other corporations

00:23:19.711 --> 00:23:21.750
because it has that data.

00:23:21.751 --> 00:23:24.420
So the collection of
data is built in to

00:23:24.421 --> 00:23:28.700
almost any business model today,
any private business model.

00:23:28.701 --> 00:23:31.364
States and
publics are also involved, but

00:23:31.365 --> 00:23:35.292
most of the data is privately
gathered and that's important.

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.194
It's also important that this,
I mean,

00:23:40.195 --> 00:23:42.815
we're not just talking about
capacities, we're talking about

00:23:42.816 --> 00:23:44.782
the values which govern
the collection of data.

00:23:44.783 --> 00:23:46.842
I was struck in
Ted Porter's book,

00:23:46.843 --> 00:23:50.010
he discusses a debate in
the 19th century in France.

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:55.050
Where there was this statistical
association of France which said

00:23:55.051 --> 00:23:59.060
the reason we need statistics
is to defend public value

00:23:59.061 --> 00:24:00.700
against private values.

00:24:00.701 --> 00:24:02.600
If we get a picture
of the whole,

00:24:02.601 --> 00:24:06.310
which we can through statistics,
then we can answer the private

00:24:06.311 --> 00:24:09.160
interests who want to dominate
society one way or the other.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:12.700
I think it's obvious that
that is radically different

00:24:12.701 --> 00:24:15.300
from the official goals of
collection of the data today.

00:24:15.301 --> 00:24:17.211
Although there are exceptions
which I'll come back to.

00:24:19.946 --> 00:24:24.400
Another key point is the opacity
of gathering knowledge today.

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:28.519
This I'm sure there are many
things which remain obscure to

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:31.716
people about the statistics in
the 19th century obviously.

00:24:31.717 --> 00:24:36.087
But in broad terms, these were
information that was gathered

00:24:36.088 --> 00:24:41.230
publicly through public means
and was able open public debate.

00:24:41.231 --> 00:24:44.500
So insurers had a few means on
the basis of which they wrote

00:24:44.501 --> 00:24:47.980
the premiums for people
wanting insurance policies.

00:24:47.981 --> 00:24:50.800
And when it became obviously
known that they gave

00:24:50.801 --> 00:24:54.172
different premiums to black
people versus white people,

00:24:54.173 --> 00:24:57.473
it wasn't a very difficult
inference to say that race had

00:24:57.474 --> 00:24:58.943
something to do with it.

00:24:58.944 --> 00:25:01.820
And there were fierce contests
in the United States about that.

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:05.489
There were not so
many variables involved.

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:08.610
Today, there are disputes
about this things.

00:25:08.611 --> 00:25:11.690
But as we know it's not so
easy to challenge.

00:25:11.691 --> 00:25:15.710
Why in a particular way
an algorithm has presented this

00:25:15.711 --> 00:25:18.030
in your news feed today and

00:25:18.031 --> 00:25:20.140
presented that in your
news feed yesterday.

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:22.240
And.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.760
Also this lead to very
interesting new debates about

00:25:26.761 --> 00:25:28.090
things which were
inherently public.

00:25:28.091 --> 00:25:29.510
What is poverty?

00:25:29.511 --> 00:25:31.870
That was a notion that emerged
after gathering various

00:25:31.871 --> 00:25:33.150
statistical information,

00:25:33.151 --> 00:25:36.320
aggregating it to talk
about a new thing, poverty,

00:25:36.321 --> 00:25:41.300
as something which is socially
determined, socially shaped.

00:25:41.301 --> 00:25:45.290
Not just down to fate,
not just curable by charity.

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:49.300
Something that could
socially be attacked

00:25:49.301 --> 00:25:52.860
because it was understood
statistically.

00:25:52.861 --> 00:25:55.760
And that produced public debates
about the outputs of data which

00:25:55.761 --> 00:25:56.960
is very different, I think,

00:25:56.961 --> 00:26:00.170
from what Frank Pasquale,
in his recent book, talks of

00:26:00.171 --> 00:26:02.709
is the generation of data
behind us or the one we mirror.

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:08.530
I talked about Booth's
poverty map before.

00:26:08.531 --> 00:26:11.600
And, of course, we know that,
although, in the past, too,

00:26:11.601 --> 00:26:14.508
data was used to discriminate
against people of course.

00:26:14.509 --> 00:26:15.610
There was also,

00:26:15.611 --> 00:26:19.340
the main emphasis was on
social generalizations,

00:26:19.341 --> 00:26:23.002
trying to understand society
itself, that goes on today.

00:26:23.003 --> 00:26:27.990
But, as we know, the principle
purpose of data collection today

00:26:27.991 --> 00:26:30.980
is to make differentiations,
as data always does.

00:26:30.981 --> 00:26:33.920
And to do it to change the
actions which corporations can

00:26:33.921 --> 00:26:38.020
take, which is to discriminate,
discriminating particularities.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:41.400
It's true that some of the
people in the marketing industry

00:26:41.401 --> 00:26:43.550
and the data industry will
tell you something different.

00:26:43.551 --> 00:26:47.270
That's a quote from a recent
book on the marketing industry.

00:26:47.271 --> 00:26:49.850
But the truth very soon will
be present in everything.

00:26:49.851 --> 00:26:53.260
If we're all wearing devices
which are measuring everything

00:26:53.261 --> 00:26:55.120
about us,
communicating with other things.

00:26:55.121 --> 00:26:58.430
The truth will be right there,
easily accessible.

00:26:58.431 --> 00:27:00.090
You'll know everything
about yourself and

00:27:00.091 --> 00:27:02.120
your loved ones if you opt in.

00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:07.740
Actually, I think that's
a very misleading view about

00:27:07.741 --> 00:27:08.670
the development.

00:27:08.671 --> 00:27:11.430
Actually we're talking
about much more particular

00:27:11.431 --> 00:27:13.360
stories today.

00:27:13.361 --> 00:27:15.650
And I think it's interesting,
again,

00:27:15.651 --> 00:27:17.730
we go back to
the public-private thing.

00:27:17.731 --> 00:27:20.120
If you know anything about
19th century and statistics,

00:27:20.121 --> 00:27:24.060
one of the key debates at that
time, in the public realm,

00:27:24.061 --> 00:27:26.020
it even reached
Charles Dickens' novel,

00:27:26.021 --> 00:27:29.350
Hard Times,
was the question of freedom.

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.840
If it was true there
was an average man,

00:27:32.841 --> 00:27:37.300
as they called it, a statistical
norm, where was free will?

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.650
Some people argued that
there was no free will.

00:27:41.651 --> 00:27:45.160
That we were condemned just to
play out the average just in our

00:27:45.161 --> 00:27:46.510
particular bodies.

00:27:46.511 --> 00:27:50.220
Others argue fiercely that
the individual remained free, we

00:27:50.221 --> 00:27:54.060
were just talking a statistical
pattern at the general level.

00:27:54.061 --> 00:27:56.262
This was one of the biggest
debates about statistics,

00:27:56.263 --> 00:27:58.340
which were about one of the most
fundamental questions in

00:27:58.341 --> 00:27:59.470
philosophy, free will.

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:04.970
I don't yet see debates about
the significance of big data

00:28:04.971 --> 00:28:07.000
on that philosophical and
normative level.

00:28:07.001 --> 00:28:09.400
But those were normal
in the 19th century,

00:28:09.401 --> 00:28:11.780
and that's an interesting
difference.

00:28:11.781 --> 00:28:13.110
But it leads to another

00:28:13.111 --> 00:28:15.110
point about what I
call conditionality.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:17.130
Of course,

00:28:17.131 --> 00:28:20.980
the state could compel you to
answer the census in a way.

00:28:20.981 --> 00:28:24.626
In a sense, we're compelled to
give in certain information when

00:28:24.627 --> 00:28:26.096
we wanna join a platform.

00:28:26.097 --> 00:28:29.460
But I think that
conditionality was different.

00:28:29.461 --> 00:28:31.370
As I said this was
much less frequent,

00:28:31.371 --> 00:28:33.930
the data, the gathering of data.

00:28:33.931 --> 00:28:37.490
And also, it's now part of
an individual deal we all do.

00:28:37.491 --> 00:28:40.980
To get access to a platform,
type of information,

00:28:40.981 --> 00:28:43.110
we just have to give a certain
amount of information.

00:28:43.111 --> 00:28:47.050
And we consent to certain
terms and conditions.

00:28:47.051 --> 00:28:50.760
It's almost a very pervasive
conditionality that's extremely

00:28:50.761 --> 00:28:52.860
hard to opt out of.

00:28:52.861 --> 00:28:57.960
And it has consequences for
right across our lives in a way

00:28:57.961 --> 00:29:01.500
that the gathering of data by
the 19th century state did not.

00:29:01.501 --> 00:29:03.000
Because the state
was much weaker.

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:06.440
That's the general comparison,

00:29:06.441 --> 00:29:09.370
let's just move on to the sort
of questions that emergent,

00:29:09.371 --> 00:29:12.190
what I'm calling the problem
of knowledge today.

00:29:12.191 --> 00:29:14.490
The first sort of other thing
is, which I can't answer,

00:29:14.491 --> 00:29:18.010
of course, because no one can
yet, is what sort of social

00:29:18.011 --> 00:29:21.740
will this new model of
social knowledge production?

00:29:21.741 --> 00:29:25.550
What sort of social will
it produce in the long run?

00:29:25.551 --> 00:29:28.590
Will there be a parallel to what
we had in the 19th century that

00:29:28.591 --> 00:29:31.510
will invent a totally new
concept like poverty?

00:29:31.511 --> 00:29:33.960
To radically see into
the social world,

00:29:33.961 --> 00:29:37.686
change the politics
of the social.

00:29:37.687 --> 00:29:41.185
Well, with so many sources of
knowledge in private hands I'm

00:29:41.186 --> 00:29:43.170
not seeing many
signs of that yet.

00:29:43.171 --> 00:29:44.420
It is a valid question,

00:29:44.421 --> 00:29:48.270
we don't know what sort of
social will be generated.

00:29:48.271 --> 00:29:51.110
But we can be sure
a different social

00:29:51.111 --> 00:29:54.190
will be generated from this very
new type of social knowledge.

00:29:55.510 --> 00:29:58.380
That raises questions of
legitimacy, which are, again,

00:29:58.381 --> 00:29:59.320
unfathomable.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:02.650
No social knowledge
can be generated and

00:30:02.651 --> 00:30:04.790
be effective unless
people trust it.

00:30:04.791 --> 00:30:09.650
And people trust and respect
the authority of those gathering

00:30:09.651 --> 00:30:12.970
the data to do so and the way
they use categorizations.

00:30:12.971 --> 00:30:15.570
There are a lot of disputes
about the way certain algorithms

00:30:15.571 --> 00:30:18.250
work right now,
particularly in US politics.

00:30:19.570 --> 00:30:21.490
We don't know
the consequences of those for

00:30:21.491 --> 00:30:24.130
the legitimacy of algorithmic
knowledge, generally.

00:30:24.131 --> 00:30:26.730
But it's a serious question we
have to think about if we think

00:30:26.731 --> 00:30:29.030
about all of this from
a social point of view.

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:33.710
Third question is, is this
really social knowledge at all?

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:38.860
Something that strikes me, I'm
officially a social theorist,

00:30:38.861 --> 00:30:39.930
whatever that is.

00:30:39.931 --> 00:30:42.440
But then, I often think, well,
Mark Zuckerberg is the highest

00:30:42.441 --> 00:30:44.050
paid social theorist
in the world.

00:30:44.051 --> 00:30:46.710
And I'm never gonna get
close to his rewards.

00:30:46.711 --> 00:30:50.640
Because the branding
of Facebook is based

00:30:50.641 --> 00:30:53.610
around social
theoretical claims.

00:30:53.611 --> 00:30:56.530
There's a whole website
where Zuckerberg's

00:30:56.531 --> 00:31:00.190
very interesting and,
in many ways, impressive or rich

00:31:00.191 --> 00:31:04.110
comments on what Facebook is
trying to do, is gathered there.

00:31:04.111 --> 00:31:08.620
And here's one particular
one where he said that,

00:31:08.621 --> 00:31:12.500
I think it was around 2010, when
they were developing the social

00:31:12.501 --> 00:31:14.332
graph and developing
that to a higher level.

00:31:14.333 --> 00:31:17.320
He said we believe that
eventually almost all apps you

00:31:17.321 --> 00:31:18.640
use are gonna be social.

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:22.220
But in the world,
that's the social world,

00:31:22.221 --> 00:31:24.770
I guess, there's a natural
spectrum from behaviors of

00:31:24.771 --> 00:31:27.890
the really naturally social
to ones that aren't.

00:31:27.891 --> 00:31:30.170
And the ones that are naturally
social are gonna be the ones

00:31:30.171 --> 00:31:31.205
that turn to social apps.

00:31:31.206 --> 00:31:36.030
So if there's an app for it,
then it's naturally social.

00:31:36.031 --> 00:31:38.680
But he's also saying
we're engineering this,

00:31:38.681 --> 00:31:40.500
otherwise it wouldn't happen.

00:31:40.501 --> 00:31:43.849
So you see there's a very
strange sort of bifurcated

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:46.090
reasoning here,
which is necessary for

00:31:46.091 --> 00:31:48.950
the brand that has to
talk about the social.

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:52.094
I'm gonna rush a bit here,

00:31:52.095 --> 00:31:54.170
cuz I want to go on
to another problem.

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:56.970
There are some ethical
problems here.

00:31:58.140 --> 00:32:00.300
A lot of data is used
to modulate behavior.

00:32:00.301 --> 00:32:03.400
There are some signs now
developing, there was

00:32:03.401 --> 00:32:06.160
a big Guardian article recently,
about those within the data

00:32:06.161 --> 00:32:08.430
industry who are becoming
a little worried about this.

00:32:08.431 --> 00:32:13.570
You may have heard of B.J. Fogg,
the Stanford data scientist.

00:32:13.571 --> 00:32:16.200
His science of captology,

00:32:16.201 --> 00:32:19.330
which is gathering data at
the micro level about people in

00:32:19.331 --> 00:32:22.740
order to seriously
influence their behavior.

00:32:22.741 --> 00:32:25.610
To nudge them regularly,
so much so

00:32:25.611 --> 00:32:27.080
that they never
forget the nudge.

00:32:27.081 --> 00:32:29.360
And they, like a ball
in a pinball machine,

00:32:29.361 --> 00:32:33.270
will regularly
achieve certain ends.

00:32:33.271 --> 00:32:34.610
But Justin Harris,

00:32:34.611 --> 00:32:37.750
who worked with him, originally,
raises this question

00:32:37.751 --> 00:32:39.820
in a magazine that's
published by the Economist.

00:32:39.821 --> 00:32:43.640
What responsibility comes
with the ability to influence

00:32:43.641 --> 00:32:45.650
the psychology of
a billion people?

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:49.480
Where's the hippocratic oath?

00:32:49.481 --> 00:32:52.900
I mean, do we have an ethics of
this influence on this scale?

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.662
Which raises another issue about
the ethics of the gathering of

00:32:57.663 --> 00:32:58.960
the data.

00:32:58.961 --> 00:33:00.510
Should there be apps for
everything?

00:33:01.644 --> 00:33:04.270
The principal of in
equals all of big data,

00:33:04.271 --> 00:33:08.820
that you must have as much data
as possible on every conceivable

00:33:08.821 --> 00:33:11.940
dimension to bring it together
to allow the computing to take

00:33:11.941 --> 00:33:16.490
place hits against basic
ethical questions.

00:33:16.491 --> 00:33:20.449
Should we have an app for
whether I'm truly grieving my

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:24.678
mother, or whether I'm really
in love with this person.

00:33:24.679 --> 00:33:28.058
Are we already stepping into
territory which is beyond

00:33:28.059 --> 00:33:29.092
unacceptable?

00:33:29.093 --> 00:33:30.250
And if so, why?

00:33:30.251 --> 00:33:32.250
Why is that unacceptable?

00:33:32.251 --> 00:33:34.100
Are we clear about why?

00:33:34.101 --> 00:33:36.990
Well, are there
questions of intimacy

00:33:36.991 --> 00:33:40.190
which we would not allow
data collection to get into?

00:33:40.191 --> 00:33:41.920
We don't know
the answer to this.

00:33:41.921 --> 00:33:43.964
So I'm just raising
these as questions.

00:33:43.965 --> 00:33:46.696
And this takes me to the second
big problem, which I will try

00:33:46.697 --> 00:33:50.070
and deal with in five minutes,
to allow for further questions.

00:33:50.071 --> 00:33:51.330
Which is the problem of freedom?

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:58.140
Obviously, big data depends on
continuous tracking of people.

00:33:58.141 --> 00:34:00.360
I don't need to
elaborate that point.

00:34:00.361 --> 00:34:03.876
Kevin Kelly, the West Coast
Tech Evangelist, says in his

00:34:03.877 --> 00:34:07.894
new book, The Inevitable, that
tracking is just one of those 12

00:34:07.895 --> 00:34:11.054
dimensions of everything
that is now inevitable and

00:34:11.055 --> 00:34:13.026
will constitute the new world.

00:34:15.344 --> 00:34:18.403
I think there's a problem with
this because what he never talks

00:34:18.404 --> 00:34:21.680
about is that tracking is not
just a technical possibility.

00:34:21.681 --> 00:34:23.380
It's a social process.

00:34:23.381 --> 00:34:26.880
As all social knowledge
production is social.

00:34:26.881 --> 00:34:29.380
The Economist,
World Economic Forum and

00:34:29.381 --> 00:34:31.610
others talk about social data.

00:34:31.611 --> 00:34:33.140
All data, big data.

00:34:33.141 --> 00:34:36.611
It's just oil, as exhaust,
it's just there.

00:34:36.612 --> 00:34:39.870
But there's no thing
that is just there.

00:34:39.871 --> 00:34:42.810
Even physical nature has
to be constructed and

00:34:42.811 --> 00:34:45.730
configured in a way so
that we can get value out of it.

00:34:45.731 --> 00:34:47.670
And the same is
true of human life.

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:52.590
We have to be configured in
such a way that we can be

00:34:52.591 --> 00:34:56.700
in generative data relations
with infrastructures.

00:34:56.701 --> 00:34:58.700
So there was no data
that is just there.

00:34:58.701 --> 00:35:01.484
And that means there has to be
a social dimension which is

00:35:01.485 --> 00:35:03.796
totally absent in
Kevin Kelly's vision, and

00:35:03.797 --> 00:35:05.860
it has been since the beginning.

00:35:05.861 --> 00:35:07.630
That's from his
famous New Rules for

00:35:07.631 --> 00:35:09.800
the New Economy article in 1997.

00:35:09.801 --> 00:35:13.020
Where he described the net as
just a collective interaction,

00:35:13.021 --> 00:35:15.170
spun off of
a trillion objects and

00:35:15.171 --> 00:35:17.960
living beings linked together
through air and glass.

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:20.700
I think that's still
his view today,

00:35:20.701 --> 00:35:24.090
and the social just isn't even
in there, in the framing.

00:35:24.091 --> 00:35:25.826
And that's a big problem.

00:35:25.827 --> 00:35:28.532
The logic of N=all,
which is the big data logic,

00:35:28.533 --> 00:35:31.950
therefore has social and ethical
costs, which in principle,

00:35:31.951 --> 00:35:33.700
it cannot recognize.

00:35:33.701 --> 00:35:38.340
So we have to be able to frame
the problem in other ways, too.

00:35:38.341 --> 00:35:41.792
Which is not to not respect
the mathematics and

00:35:41.793 --> 00:35:45.333
other skills that go
into generating the data,

00:35:45.334 --> 00:35:48.447
because they're
extremely important.

00:35:48.448 --> 00:35:50.570
What could we do about this?

00:35:50.571 --> 00:35:53.680
There's a lot of debate about
outlawing bad data uses.

00:35:53.681 --> 00:35:55.960
And that's relatively
straightforward.

00:35:55.961 --> 00:35:57.381
In principle,

00:35:57.382 --> 00:36:02.639
except that one of the solutions
is often transparency.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:07.652
And as Helen Nissenbaum point
out, machine learning is so

00:36:07.653 --> 00:36:12.357
complex that there's a limit
to what we can see in order

00:36:12.358 --> 00:36:15.852
to be able to see into
machine learning.

00:36:15.853 --> 00:36:19.407
We can't fully see into it
because it is a non-linear

00:36:19.408 --> 00:36:23.120
process which feed backs on
itself in ways that cannot be

00:36:23.121 --> 00:36:26.190
predicted and
can't be seen into.

00:36:26.191 --> 00:36:27.770
So there may be problems there.

00:36:27.771 --> 00:36:30.240
But they're not what
I wanna focus on.

00:36:30.241 --> 00:36:35.040
Some people talk about the
effects of continuous tracking

00:36:35.041 --> 00:36:38.900
of people, making them feel
lacking anxious, lacking in

00:36:38.901 --> 00:36:44.520
confidence, just in East Germany
before 1989, the life of others.

00:36:44.521 --> 00:36:48.378
Well, that's a serious
worry as well.

00:36:48.379 --> 00:36:49.940
And there are power
relations there, but

00:36:49.941 --> 00:36:51.464
I do think it's
the fundamental worry.

00:36:51.465 --> 00:36:53.020
And this is what I
want to focus on.

00:36:53.021 --> 00:36:56.310
For me, the fundamental issues,
and it's the one least talked

00:36:56.311 --> 00:37:01.500
about right now, is the very
fact of gathering data itself.

00:37:01.501 --> 00:37:03.640
That's not to say that all
data gathering is bad.

00:37:03.641 --> 00:37:05.460
Of course, I'm not saying that.

00:37:05.461 --> 00:37:09.800
But what we're dealing with
is moving to a social order

00:37:09.801 --> 00:37:14.680
which is based primarily
on the continuous gathering

00:37:14.681 --> 00:37:17.980
of data from individuals.

00:37:17.981 --> 00:37:19.310
And the question is,

00:37:19.311 --> 00:37:23.360
is this fully compatible with
what we think are our values or

00:37:23.361 --> 00:37:26.400
is there a deep tench that
we have to take seriously?

00:37:26.401 --> 00:37:28.420
And I think there is a tench.

00:37:28.421 --> 00:37:31.260
Let me immediately say, there's
some type of data collection

00:37:31.261 --> 00:37:33.660
that we might
generally want to have.

00:37:33.661 --> 00:37:35.646
Even on that vast scale.

00:37:35.647 --> 00:37:39.600
In the health area, many people
argue that we simply have to

00:37:39.601 --> 00:37:43.808
start gathering our health data
to deal with serious disease.

00:37:43.809 --> 00:37:48.563
And I take those arguments
really seriously.

00:37:48.564 --> 00:37:51.766
Other people argue that it's
better to know how one's body is

00:37:51.767 --> 00:37:53.552
functioning on a micro level,
and

00:37:53.553 --> 00:37:56.210
it's good to have wearables and
so on and so forth.

00:37:56.211 --> 00:37:58.080
I don't dispute that.

00:37:58.081 --> 00:38:01.830
The question is, what happens
when this becomes the norm?

00:38:01.831 --> 00:38:03.620
And when it even
becomes the requirement

00:38:03.621 --> 00:38:07.440
of insurance companies,
employers, friends, and so on.

00:38:07.441 --> 00:38:10.940
That's a very different social
order from where I just choose

00:38:10.941 --> 00:38:13.610
to understand my body better
as I try to run faster.

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:16.720
That's a radically
different possibility.

00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:22.400
Even in the health case and
voluntary self-tracking,

00:38:22.401 --> 00:38:24.640
there are major issues
about anonymization.

00:38:24.641 --> 00:38:26.740
The data are formally
anonymized.

00:38:26.741 --> 00:38:29.090
But there's a lot of debate in
the health literature as whether

00:38:29.091 --> 00:38:31.480
anonymization is
actually possible.

00:38:31.481 --> 00:38:34.230
So even here,
even in these special cases,

00:38:34.231 --> 00:38:37.380
there are worries
about data collection.

00:38:37.381 --> 00:38:39.730
There are even fundamental
issues about whether certain

00:38:39.731 --> 00:38:43.170
data must always be under
the control of the individual.

00:38:43.171 --> 00:38:48.060
The philosopher Daniel Sulmasy
argues that the person's genome

00:38:48.061 --> 00:38:52.860
is so fundamental to who they
are and who or what they can be,

00:38:52.861 --> 00:38:56.915
that it must only be under
the control of the individual.

00:38:56.916 --> 00:39:00.150
Licensable if you like,
to the wider realm.

00:39:00.151 --> 00:39:03.830
The dangers are so great of
allowing it to be in control

00:39:03.831 --> 00:39:06.670
anything other than that
specific individual.

00:39:06.671 --> 00:39:07.800
That's an interesting question.

00:39:07.801 --> 00:39:11.162
That's a sort of fundamental,
if you like, to data collection,

00:39:11.163 --> 00:39:13.873
and data circulation,
which you might think about.

00:39:13.874 --> 00:39:16.305
So starting to wrap up.

00:39:16.306 --> 00:39:19.643
There were clearly ethical
tensions, political tensions,

00:39:19.644 --> 00:39:20.676
developing here.

00:39:20.677 --> 00:39:25.029
For me, the real contradiction
is between the shift to

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:28.625
an order based around
continuous tracking,

00:39:28.626 --> 00:39:31.380
which is where we
seem to be going.

00:39:31.381 --> 00:39:35.578
And the notion of freedom or
autonomy, which we normally

00:39:35.579 --> 00:39:39.170
think is at the basis of
why we are democracies.

00:39:39.171 --> 00:39:42.260
Why we pride ourselves
on being free societies.

00:39:42.261 --> 00:39:43.810
Why we would even
want democracy.

00:39:45.210 --> 00:39:47.710
Without going into any detail,
for me, and I've looked for

00:39:47.711 --> 00:39:50.440
a long time, the only
philosophical reference point I

00:39:50.441 --> 00:39:54.510
found that helps me at all
here,tTo get right to the core

00:39:54.511 --> 00:39:56.800
is Hegel's notion of,
he called it freedom, but

00:39:56.801 --> 00:39:59.100
it's really autonomy
that we would say now.

00:39:59.101 --> 00:40:02.270
Which he defined this way,
very interestingly.

00:40:02.271 --> 00:40:08.380
Freedom is the ability to be
with oneself in the other.

00:40:08.381 --> 00:40:10.270
It took me six months
to understand that.

00:40:10.271 --> 00:40:11.570
So let me give you a shortcut.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:16.190
He gets absolutely, in the
beginning of the 19th century,

00:40:16.191 --> 00:40:18.950
that identity is relational.

00:40:18.951 --> 00:40:20.170
Freedom is relational.

00:40:20.171 --> 00:40:21.890
It emerges in social elections.

00:40:21.891 --> 00:40:23.810
There's a material
infrastructure.

00:40:23.811 --> 00:40:26.220
That could include
some data collection.

00:40:26.221 --> 00:40:28.218
That's in the other.

00:40:28.219 --> 00:40:33.067
But that necessity to be with
one's self, to have the ability

00:40:33.068 --> 00:40:38.198
to reflect continuously in one's
life about where one is going,

00:40:38.199 --> 00:40:42.116
what one's done wrong,
what one's life means,

00:40:42.117 --> 00:40:45.312
in dialogue continuously
with others.

00:40:45.313 --> 00:40:48.530
But always,
it being one's own reflection.

00:40:48.531 --> 00:40:51.470
No other entity listening
in at every point or

00:40:51.471 --> 00:40:53.170
directing that reflection.

00:40:53.171 --> 00:40:56.390
That, for him, is the core
of what human beings are.

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:01.360
That is the eaftos, the self,
eaftos the Greek word.

00:41:01.361 --> 00:41:05.040
That is the thing whose
freedom we're talking about.

00:41:05.041 --> 00:41:08.550
So what we're talking about
here is not a danger for

00:41:08.551 --> 00:41:11.620
a certain capacity or
a dimension,

00:41:11.621 --> 00:41:14.320
we're talking about the very
possibility of being a self.

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:19.920
And that, for me, is what's
at stake if we go without any

00:41:19.921 --> 00:41:24.640
qualification or debate down
the path of continuous tracking.

00:41:24.641 --> 00:41:26.330
And that's why I'm
worried about it.

00:41:26.331 --> 00:41:27.950
There's biology here.

00:41:27.951 --> 00:41:31.640
Even not very complex animals,
worms and so on,

00:41:31.641 --> 00:41:34.710
need a certain space
which they defend.

00:41:38.910 --> 00:41:41.620
European data law is very
much related to this.

00:41:41.621 --> 00:41:44.973
Julie Cohen has
written about this.

00:41:44.974 --> 00:41:47.518
I won't allow time for
questions, I'll just leave that

00:41:47.519 --> 00:41:49.730
question hanging and
say well I've done so far.

00:41:49.731 --> 00:41:52.320
I think we've reached
the point where we have big

00:41:52.321 --> 00:41:55.500
problems about the production
social knowledge and

00:41:55.501 --> 00:41:56.990
the condition of
that production.

00:41:56.991 --> 00:42:01.825
Which creates tension with core
values, such as freedom and

00:42:01.826 --> 00:42:04.400
autonomy, which we simply
have to start debating.

00:42:04.401 --> 00:42:07.230
And that means thinking about
all of this as a social process,

00:42:07.231 --> 00:42:09.600
which we are involved
in as social beings.

00:42:09.601 --> 00:42:11.610
We have a voice in this.

00:42:11.611 --> 00:42:12.960
It is really
important therefore,

00:42:12.961 --> 00:42:16.510
that we bring together those
that produce social knowledge

00:42:16.511 --> 00:42:20.551
validly, smartly, importantly.

00:42:20.552 --> 00:42:24.120
We've those, any of us, it could
include all of us in this room

00:42:24.121 --> 00:42:27.030
and anyone who's interested
in what the cost of that.

00:42:27.031 --> 00:42:29.305
The externalities if you like,

00:42:29.306 --> 00:42:33.012
might be when transposed to
the full social scale and

00:42:33.013 --> 00:42:36.260
accumulated over time,
over decades.

00:42:36.261 --> 00:42:39.220
That's the issue, and I think
we need to be debating it.

00:42:39.221 --> 00:42:40.420
Ways forward,

00:42:40.421 --> 00:42:42.860
the law is obviously
one very important way.

00:42:42.861 --> 00:42:47.020
The European general data
protection regulations is very

00:42:47.021 --> 00:42:49.930
interesting because it actually
starts from human rights.

00:42:49.931 --> 00:42:52.770
But there's Kate Crawford and
colleagues writing out of

00:42:52.771 --> 00:42:57.010
Microsoft New York lab made
a very interesting point.

00:42:57.011 --> 00:43:00.440
You can have the best legal
framework you want, but

00:43:00.441 --> 00:43:04.100
if it operates in a field of
force where an insurer or

00:43:04.101 --> 00:43:10.670
employer requires you to sign
away your data, for a perfectly

00:43:10.671 --> 00:43:16.110
valid conditions of running
their employment, or selling

00:43:16.111 --> 00:43:20.670
you an insurance policy, that
regulation will not help you.

00:43:20.671 --> 00:43:22.100
It's not designed to help you.

00:43:22.101 --> 00:43:24.220
It's not designed to
interrupt business,

00:43:24.221 --> 00:43:27.350
it's designed to
induce certain checks.

00:43:27.351 --> 00:43:29.964
But if the whole push is to

00:43:29.965 --> 00:43:35.660
quasi-enforce the giving up of
data, then it won't help us.

00:43:35.661 --> 00:43:37.680
So we have to think
about other things.

00:43:37.681 --> 00:43:38.480
In the Health Sector,

00:43:38.481 --> 00:43:41.670
Barbara Evans,
one of the most brilliant legal

00:43:41.671 --> 00:43:45.285
theorists in the health sector
talks about getting together.

00:43:45.286 --> 00:43:46.490
This relates to work,

00:43:46.491 --> 00:43:49.740
seriously having a collective
conversation about the different

00:43:49.741 --> 00:43:52.990
data collection infrastructure
that would help us

00:43:52.991 --> 00:43:55.930
bring the problems to the
program, to talk about them, and

00:43:55.931 --> 00:43:58.580
try to find collective solutions
to deal with these words.

00:43:58.581 --> 00:43:59.400
I think that's a really

00:43:59.401 --> 00:44:01.590
interesting example
from the health sector.

00:44:01.591 --> 00:44:03.351
But she doesn't come
up with solutions,

00:44:03.352 --> 00:44:05.628
she just proposes
the possibility, it's not easy.

00:44:05.629 --> 00:44:09.313
And finally, I think, and I draw
this partly from Julie Cummings

00:44:09.314 --> 00:44:12.998
work, we do have to, without in
anyway challenging the validity

00:44:12.999 --> 00:44:16.420
of collecting data and using
it to provide smart solutions.

00:44:16.421 --> 00:44:19.190
We have to raise the
philosophical question, as I've

00:44:19.191 --> 00:44:23.920
said before, that the principle
of any which is valid and

00:44:23.921 --> 00:44:25.960
interesting mathematically,

00:44:25.961 --> 00:44:31.100
must at some point cut across
our ethical limits and say no,

00:44:31.101 --> 00:44:34.910
you will not have that data,
you must not collect that data.

00:44:34.911 --> 00:44:36.500
Because if you collect that,

00:44:36.501 --> 00:44:39.700
you damage the integrity of
what I am, and what you are.

00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:43.760
And that's why I think it's
an interesting problem,

00:44:43.761 --> 00:44:45.890
that we've just got to
think about a lot more.

00:44:45.891 --> 00:44:47.130
So thank you for listening.

00:44:47.131 --> 00:44:49.710
Sorry, I waxed went on
slightly beyond my time.

00:44:49.711 --> 00:44:51.590
But I hope we have time for
questions, and

00:44:51.591 --> 00:44:53.416
don't have to finish
exactly at five.

00:44:53.417 --> 00:44:55.477
That's that.

00:44:55.478 --> 00:44:58.850
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; In case you wanted to go

00:44:58.851 --> 00:44:59.380
back to a slide.

00:44:59.381 --> 00:45:01.618
&gt;&gt; So we actually have ten more
minutes for questions, but

00:45:01.619 --> 00:45:03.914
of course we will take your
questions as you give it to us.

00:45:03.915 --> 00:45:06.374
&gt;&gt; Sorry, I have so much to say.

00:45:06.375 --> 00:45:08.192
&gt;&gt; But
I will let you [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:08.193 --> 00:45:11.560
&gt;&gt; Cool. Okay.

00:45:11.561 --> 00:45:13.950
&gt;&gt; So a lot of your discussion

00:45:13.951 --> 00:45:16.550
made me think of FA Hyatt's

00:45:18.070 --> 00:45:20.570
sort of discussion of
the problems of statistics.

00:45:20.571 --> 00:45:23.080
So he has this vision in which

00:45:23.081 --> 00:45:26.090
the problem with statistics
would be attempt to

00:45:26.091 --> 00:45:28.750
essentially organize
an economy using statistics.

00:45:28.751 --> 00:45:29.650
Is it statistics or

00:45:29.651 --> 00:45:33.460
these aggregate course
things that don't pick

00:45:33.461 --> 00:45:34.900
up the particularity of-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:45:34.901 --> 00:45:36.870
&gt;&gt; Individual situations and

00:45:36.871 --> 00:45:39.610
that the beauty of
the market allegedly.

00:45:39.611 --> 00:45:42.960
It's that it delegates to
quote the man on the spot

00:45:42.961 --> 00:45:44.810
who has this much
more granular data.

00:45:46.750 --> 00:45:50.300
It seems like if you substitute
the word market there for

00:45:50.301 --> 00:45:55.670
sort of the autonomous free
society, or something like that.

00:45:55.671 --> 00:45:59.470
Then, the idea is that once you
start getting that granularity

00:45:59.471 --> 00:46:02.240
collected in this automated way,

00:46:02.241 --> 00:46:05.820
either individual
cells get delocalized.

00:46:05.821 --> 00:46:08.820
They have some identity that
is not related to their

00:46:08.821 --> 00:46:12.910
local surroundings and that the
role that plays in society, or

00:46:12.911 --> 00:46:14.650
they sort of disappear entirely.

00:46:14.651 --> 00:46:18.730
Which was sort of what you
were pointing towards.

00:46:18.731 --> 00:46:22.970
So I sort of, maybe that's
not even really a question.

00:46:22.971 --> 00:46:26.460
But it's just an interesting
[CROSSTALK] reflection.

00:46:26.461 --> 00:46:29.193
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it is
interesting, because it, first

00:46:29.194 --> 00:46:32.474
of all, because it zeroes in on
the fundamental question here,

00:46:32.475 --> 00:46:35.170
which is about, what is it
to know the social world?

00:46:35.171 --> 00:46:37.910
And that was one of the things
that drove his work,

00:46:37.911 --> 00:46:43.050
the puzzles about, obviously
that's extremely interesting.

00:46:43.051 --> 00:46:45.750
And clearly,
I'm not suggesting for

00:46:45.751 --> 00:46:48.020
one moment that somehow
statistics are for

00:46:48.021 --> 00:46:51.050
better knowledge of the social,
compared to what we have today.

00:46:51.051 --> 00:46:53.150
I'm just looking at
the social externalities,

00:46:53.151 --> 00:46:56.480
the costs of this model and
that's where my focus is.

00:46:56.481 --> 00:46:59.930
There are many arguments to
say that we will understand

00:46:59.931 --> 00:47:02.320
if we go down entirely this way,

00:47:02.321 --> 00:47:04.430
many aspects of the social
better than before.

00:47:04.431 --> 00:47:07.910
And that's what psychology is
being driven by today and so on.

00:47:07.911 --> 00:47:10.590
That incidentally is why I
find those that work for

00:47:10.591 --> 00:47:13.820
example with BJ Fog at
Stanford who have been through

00:47:13.821 --> 00:47:17.280
the very smartest learning of
what you can do with analytics,

00:47:17.281 --> 00:47:19.940
to predict behavior with
extraordinary precision.

00:47:19.941 --> 00:47:22.600
Are still are the ones
particularly troubled

00:47:22.601 --> 00:47:24.220
by where this is leading.

00:47:24.221 --> 00:47:26.210
That's already puts
one to decide there

00:47:26.211 --> 00:47:27.650
is more to debate here.

00:47:27.651 --> 00:47:29.770
But as to question of the future
itself, well, of course,

00:47:29.771 --> 00:47:30.980
that's a great point.

00:47:30.981 --> 00:47:33.520
I have no idea where
this is heading.

00:47:33.521 --> 00:47:38.730
I think Kevin Carey
implies in his writing

00:47:38.731 --> 00:47:44.020
that we should slope
off this rather 18th,

00:47:44.021 --> 00:47:47.800
19th century sense of our
particular individualities

00:47:47.801 --> 00:47:49.940
came out of a certain phase
of the enlightenment.

00:47:49.941 --> 00:47:53.350
Possessive individualism and
so on, and so forth.

00:47:53.351 --> 00:47:55.060
And move to a different phase.

00:47:55.061 --> 00:47:57.180
Jeremy Rifkin, Wharton Business,

00:47:57.181 --> 00:48:00.480
who has made a similar argument
in his zero cost some society,

00:48:00.481 --> 00:48:01.774
whatever the title
of his book is.

00:48:01.775 --> 00:48:05.410
There's a privacy literally
in it, 18th century notion,

00:48:05.411 --> 00:48:07.430
we should just get over it.

00:48:07.431 --> 00:48:09.890
But this, of course,
ignores the deep,

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:14.490
almost acultural core
of privacy to notion.

00:48:14.491 --> 00:48:18.610
Culture varies, and privacy
varies continuously, of course.

00:48:18.611 --> 00:48:22.570
So does autonomy, our sense of
what it is to be autonomous.

00:48:22.571 --> 00:48:26.620
But as I said, you only have
to go to relatively simple or

00:48:26.621 --> 00:48:28.490
complex animals.

00:48:28.491 --> 00:48:31.130
And they,
unless they are swarm animals,

00:48:31.131 --> 00:48:35.580
which is a different case,
will defend a space around

00:48:35.581 --> 00:48:37.980
the self from where
the environment begins.

00:48:37.981 --> 00:48:40.430
That is the space
of that entity.

00:48:40.431 --> 00:48:42.320
We're talking, if you like,
about, I think,

00:48:42.321 --> 00:48:45.940
that at the core rule of
the discussions of privacy,

00:48:45.941 --> 00:48:48.050
autonomy, with all
the confusions and

00:48:48.051 --> 00:48:49.970
messiness which is a problem.

00:48:49.971 --> 00:48:54.200
There's a certain minimal notion
of the integrity of the self

00:48:55.210 --> 00:48:57.830
which I would defy anyone to
say we can dispense with and

00:48:57.831 --> 00:48:59.590
still be a self.

00:48:59.591 --> 00:49:05.150
It's true we can move into
a collective being, but

00:49:05.151 --> 00:49:08.500
I have yet to meet anyone
who actually wants

00:49:09.850 --> 00:49:12.530
that when we think
about the costs.

00:49:12.531 --> 00:49:13.550
We could have the debate and

00:49:13.551 --> 00:49:15.210
there's a sense that
one can't debate it.

00:49:15.211 --> 00:49:19.740
It will be a fundamental
phase shift in humanity, but

00:49:19.741 --> 00:49:22.800
I don't think we're at least
ready to get there yet

00:49:22.801 --> 00:49:24.050
even if we tell ourselves.

00:49:24.051 --> 00:49:25.185
Yes, good to see you.

00:49:25.186 --> 00:49:29.654
&gt;&gt; Listening to you,
it's a very dystopian view

00:49:29.655 --> 00:49:34.850
of the history where
humans created business.

00:49:34.851 --> 00:49:37.414
At first they were
bakeries of little mom and

00:49:37.415 --> 00:49:40.878
pop businesses and they served
us, and they grew, and they

00:49:40.879 --> 00:49:44.622
grew, and then they turned into
contemporary corporations and

00:49:44.623 --> 00:49:48.225
what we're seeing is some of the
takeover of human autonomy by

00:49:48.226 --> 00:49:49.652
the world of business.

00:49:49.653 --> 00:49:53.709
It feels like fundamentally,
it's not simply an issue around

00:49:53.710 --> 00:49:57.687
surveillance, per se, but that
this is, when you talk about

00:49:57.688 --> 00:50:01.361
it being private entities as
opposed to the government,

00:50:01.362 --> 00:50:05.417
that somebody's ostensibly doing
it For the public that these

00:50:05.418 --> 00:50:09.421
are these private entities that
society has given birth too.

00:50:09.422 --> 00:50:10.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:50:10.001 --> 00:50:12.750
&gt;&gt; That are turning
on the people and

00:50:12.751 --> 00:50:13.901
sort of devouring humans.

00:50:13.902 --> 00:50:15.334
&gt;&gt; That's not what I
was saying at all.

00:50:15.335 --> 00:50:18.576
&gt;&gt; No, no, but it's a feeling
that comes out a little bit.

00:50:18.577 --> 00:50:21.065
And that's-
&gt;&gt; I'm happy to respond.

00:50:21.066 --> 00:50:23.959
&gt;&gt; And it's the model and
prevalence of the private and

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:26.010
when you talk about
the way forward.

00:50:26.011 --> 00:50:28.300
I think an interesting way of
thinking about it is to look at

00:50:28.301 --> 00:50:31.790
what are some of the big
problems we want solved?

00:50:31.791 --> 00:50:32.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So an obvious one

00:50:32.671 --> 00:50:34.340
you mentioned was health,
which says, well,

00:50:34.341 --> 00:50:37.340
maybe if insurance agencies
are collecting all of this data,

00:50:37.341 --> 00:50:41.260
they have to use that data for
health.

00:50:41.261 --> 00:50:44.530
And to look [INAUDIBLE]
both had issues around

00:50:44.531 --> 00:50:46.250
what is it that we want to see.

00:50:46.251 --> 00:50:50.080
And into these increasingly
opaque entities and

00:50:50.081 --> 00:50:53.950
institutions, because very
little of this is about turning

00:50:53.951 --> 00:50:56.940
the observing eye
into other things.

00:50:56.941 --> 00:50:58.330
So if you, for instance,

00:50:58.331 --> 00:51:02.390
think about the environment or
think about how different.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:07.420
The unforeseen consequences
of the way that

00:51:07.421 --> 00:51:11.650
money that is given to support
certain types of farms has ended

00:51:11.651 --> 00:51:13.930
up having causing
consequences we don't want.

00:51:13.931 --> 00:51:18.253
So how do we use this type
of data to better understand

00:51:18.254 --> 00:51:22.492
these institutions
[INAUDIBLE] extremely opaque.

00:51:22.493 --> 00:51:27.487
And focus on the kind of big
challenges that may involve

00:51:27.488 --> 00:51:31.547
data but
it's not necessarily data about.

00:51:31.548 --> 00:51:34.510
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean I'm glad you
asked that because of course

00:51:34.511 --> 00:51:35.700
I tried to make it clear.

00:51:35.701 --> 00:51:38.560
But I'm not against
data collection,

00:51:38.561 --> 00:51:40.260
I'm not against data processing.

00:51:40.261 --> 00:51:42.890
I said it a few times,
that's not a problem.

00:51:42.891 --> 00:51:45.400
It's a new capacity that we have
that we need to nurture and

00:51:45.401 --> 00:51:46.680
think about.

00:51:46.681 --> 00:51:51.860
What I'm raising is a question,
which is that if I'm right or

00:51:51.861 --> 00:51:57.150
even half right, that there's a
difference in the overall social

00:51:57.151 --> 00:51:58.380
deal, if you like.

00:51:58.381 --> 00:52:01.120
Through which knowledge of
the world is now being generated

00:52:01.121 --> 00:52:04.770
compared with a valid comparison
of the 19th and 20th century,

00:52:04.771 --> 00:52:08.560
which is simply about
a question of where it goes on.

00:52:08.561 --> 00:52:10.960
The sorts of purposes for
which [INAUDIBLE] control and

00:52:10.961 --> 00:52:15.990
how far those hopefully open for
debate, visible, and so on.

00:52:15.991 --> 00:52:19.593
And that's what I was saying,
there's [INAUDIBLE] was

00:52:19.594 --> 00:52:22.187
an [INAUDIBLE] because-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:22.188 --> 00:52:23.449
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] of course,

00:52:23.450 --> 00:52:26.095
I was making an emphasis
rhetorically [INAUDIBLE] our

00:52:26.096 --> 00:52:28.030
attention but
I think you're right.

00:52:28.031 --> 00:52:29.650
So can I just come back to that?

00:52:29.651 --> 00:52:30.630
Cuz you're right, clearly.

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:36.560
That big data could be turned to
solve certain problems, which we

00:52:36.561 --> 00:52:39.770
all want to solve in ways that
could be fully accountable and

00:52:39.771 --> 00:52:41.780
useful, and
that would be wonderful.

00:52:41.781 --> 00:52:43.170
I'm just saying we're not yet

00:52:43.171 --> 00:52:46.190
at the position to have
the social architecture.

00:52:46.191 --> 00:52:49.120
To deal with the possible
externalities of the data

00:52:49.121 --> 00:52:50.710
collection and
the data processing,

00:52:50.711 --> 00:52:51.990
that's the question I'm raising.

00:52:51.991 --> 00:52:54.558
Those externalities
are fundamental and deep.

00:52:54.559 --> 00:52:58.421
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] We're still
assuming that the subject of

00:52:58.422 --> 00:53:01.367
the surveillance
is always humans.

00:53:01.368 --> 00:53:02.720
&gt;&gt; No, sure.

00:53:02.721 --> 00:53:06.730
&gt;&gt; Thinking more that there's
a lot of opaque entities

00:53:06.731 --> 00:53:10.050
that maybe we could bring
a lot more surveillance.

00:53:10.051 --> 00:53:10.810
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:53:10.811 --> 00:53:13.520
Again, I wasn't saying
surveillance of nonhuman

00:53:13.521 --> 00:53:17.040
subjects or
other entities was a problem.

00:53:17.041 --> 00:53:19.600
I was focusing on the human
problem because I think that's

00:53:19.601 --> 00:53:21.880
where we start from,
but thank you.

00:53:21.881 --> 00:53:25.530
Now then I better wrap a few
together and then try.

00:53:27.240 --> 00:53:29.748
&gt;&gt; Thank you for a wonderful and
productive talk.

00:53:29.749 --> 00:53:33.360
I was kindof struck by how,
until you had this exchange with

00:53:33.361 --> 00:53:36.620
Glen, that the word privacy
wasn't too much in here.

00:53:36.621 --> 00:53:38.229
Because it seems like a lot
of these social problems we

00:53:38.230 --> 00:53:39.315
usually put in
the privacy bucket.

00:53:39.316 --> 00:53:40.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:53:40.601 --> 00:53:43.250
&gt;&gt; But there, and especially
after the Equifax hack,

00:53:43.251 --> 00:53:45.790
when most Americans social
security numbers are now

00:53:45.791 --> 00:53:47.030
public in some form.

00:53:47.031 --> 00:53:49.280
It doesn't seem like people
get very upset about privacy,

00:53:49.281 --> 00:53:51.880
so I thought there was
something very productive about

00:53:51.881 --> 00:53:54.160
moving to the language
