stopped it, it's not clear what.

01:05:33.991 --> 01:05:35.790
Abandoning the national census,

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:39.610
about five years ago there was
a small debate about that.

01:05:39.611 --> 01:05:42.680
On the basis that far better
data was being gathered

01:05:42.681 --> 01:05:45.610
all the time by
private organizations.

01:05:45.611 --> 01:05:47.030
The state was running
out of money.

01:05:48.690 --> 01:05:53.510
Why not just defer that
fundamental faculty of the state

01:05:53.511 --> 01:05:55.900
which is to know its
population in order to govern?

01:05:57.530 --> 01:06:00.620
So I think there are very
interesting relations between

01:06:00.621 --> 01:06:01.530
this stage in part, and

01:06:01.531 --> 01:06:04.288
which raise massive
political theory questions.

01:06:04.289 --> 01:06:06.830
But you're implying the question
which goes back to Glenn's,

01:06:06.831 --> 01:06:08.415
which I think is
a really serious point.

01:06:08.416 --> 01:06:12.230
Well, couldn't we imagine that
with a different assemblage,

01:06:12.231 --> 01:06:16.870
a different configuration or
authority trust or so on, we

01:06:16.871 --> 01:06:20.340
could consent to more data being
gathered about ourselves and

01:06:20.341 --> 01:06:21.450
being stored.

01:06:21.451 --> 01:06:22.700
I don't deny that for a moment.

01:06:22.701 --> 01:06:25.950
That's why I pointedly
mentioned the health case.

01:06:25.951 --> 01:06:28.860
But that will require very
different social architecture

01:06:28.861 --> 01:06:30.330
for what we have in the moment.

01:06:30.331 --> 01:06:33.108
I know that Glenn is
exactly interested in.

01:06:33.109 --> 01:06:36.737
But I think I'm trying to point
out these extremely difficult

01:06:36.738 --> 01:06:39.819
questions to generate such
a social architecture.

01:06:39.820 --> 01:06:42.518
It's never been
done in the past.

01:06:42.519 --> 01:06:46.347
It's only been done by states
which did not successfully

01:06:46.348 --> 01:06:48.060
generate trust.

01:06:48.061 --> 01:06:50.830
We don't barely know
how to solve that

01:06:50.831 --> 01:06:53.330
higher dimensional
interdependence problem.

01:06:53.331 --> 01:06:56.027
So I'm exposing the problem,
yes it will be great if we had

01:06:56.028 --> 01:06:59.121
better solutions because we're
not gonna stop data collection.

01:06:59.122 --> 01:07:01.646
And I would love it to be
put to more positive things.

01:07:01.647 --> 01:07:05.360
But I think we're barely at
the beginning of even posing

01:07:05.361 --> 01:07:07.739
the question let
alone solving it.

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:10.490
&gt;&gt; So thank you so
much for your talk.

01:07:10.491 --> 01:07:14.545
I think what I'm struck by
[INAUDIBLE] having, just the day

01:07:14.546 --> 01:07:18.685
to day conversations about
collecting data, it's less of

01:07:18.686 --> 01:07:22.555
a n equal all, and more of
an n of everything I can get.

01:07:22.556 --> 01:07:25.198
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Precisely the interest in

01:07:25.199 --> 01:07:28.960
getting what I can without
the possibility of

01:07:28.961 --> 01:07:32.326
the disruption of
requesting consent,

01:07:32.327 --> 01:07:37.375
because that might impinge on
either the representatives,

01:07:37.376 --> 01:07:41.533
my sample, but
then this really odd disconnect.

01:07:41.534 --> 01:07:42.627
I don't know if
it's a disconnect.

01:07:42.628 --> 01:07:45.653
What do you make of
the willingness to say one,

01:07:45.654 --> 01:07:47.747
I'll work with
what I can get and

01:07:47.748 --> 01:07:50.845
I'm gonna extrapolate
out from there.

01:07:50.846 --> 01:07:53.392
It's not as though
I've ever talked with

01:07:53.393 --> 01:07:56.666
any engineer in this area
that said what we really need

01:07:56.667 --> 01:08:00.188
is a sensor on every person,
every baby, every second.

01:08:00.189 --> 01:08:02.608
None of them feel like
that's necessary,

01:08:02.609 --> 01:08:06.239
precisely because they imagine
that What they're working on is

01:08:06.240 --> 01:08:08.869
a new statistics to
extrapolate and project.

01:08:08.870 --> 01:08:10.783
&gt;&gt; In fact, the people who say
that are the computational

01:08:10.784 --> 01:08:11.286
scientists.

01:08:11.287 --> 01:08:11.795
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

01:08:11.796 --> 01:08:15.048
&gt;&gt; Who have the vision
of what if everyone was.

01:08:15.049 --> 01:08:17.562
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there's no engineer who
feels like that's necessary.

01:08:17.563 --> 01:08:21.240
And I think it perhaps goes back
to diagnosis of A certain set of

01:08:21.241 --> 01:08:24.519
assumptions about the natural
state of a human being.

01:08:24.520 --> 01:08:25.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:08:25.731 --> 01:08:28.789
&gt;&gt; So what do you make of
that thinking about the end?

01:08:28.790 --> 01:08:31.346
&gt;&gt; Well, that's obviously
very encouraging,

01:08:31.347 --> 01:08:34.040
in other words implicitly
there is resistance.

01:08:35.270 --> 01:08:37.138
I'm not claiming any special,

01:08:37.139 --> 01:08:39.290
there's a sort of
resistance here.

01:08:39.291 --> 01:08:41.840
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Resistance is unnecessary.

01:08:41.841 --> 01:08:42.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Well-

01:08:42.581 --> 01:08:44.104
&gt;&gt; Well, I don't really need

01:08:44.105 --> 01:08:47.138
a sensor on every baby,
I just need some babies.

01:08:47.139 --> 01:08:48.220
&gt;&gt; Right, right.

01:08:48.221 --> 01:08:49.349
&gt;&gt; The babies that
I could get to.

01:08:49.350 --> 01:08:52.028
I mean, if you suggested-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean-

01:08:52.029 --> 01:08:52.671
&gt;&gt; It's like,

01:08:52.672 --> 01:08:54.782
we could encourage,
we could refer them-

01:08:54.783 --> 01:08:55.941
&gt;&gt; And that, of course,

01:08:55.942 --> 01:08:58.810
is the older model of building
statistical knowledge,

01:08:58.811 --> 01:09:02.044
which didn't raise this sort of
externality problem that I'm

01:09:02.045 --> 01:09:03.180
talking about.

01:09:03.181 --> 01:09:06.201
But I think the picture
looks rather differently if

01:09:06.202 --> 01:09:09.222
you go study what our
marketers are talking about,

01:09:09.223 --> 01:09:10.987
the gathering information.

01:09:10.988 --> 01:09:14.490
Their business model is about
gathering all information at

01:09:14.491 --> 01:09:16.460
all times, and hoping to use it.

01:09:16.461 --> 01:09:19.515
For example, there's a
interesting report on the future

01:09:19.516 --> 01:09:22.631
of wearables by Price Waterhouse
Cooper, came out three or

01:09:22.632 --> 01:09:24.050
four years ago.

01:09:24.051 --> 01:09:27.921
And Joe Thoreau talks about it
in his book on a shopping mall

01:09:27.922 --> 01:09:29.272
and surveillance.

01:09:29.273 --> 01:09:32.710
Which seriously, without
irony or humor suggested that

01:09:32.711 --> 01:09:35.860
if everyone started to have
a wearable built in which

01:09:35.861 --> 01:09:39.083
was continuously tracking
the state of their body and

01:09:39.084 --> 01:09:42.451
continuously matched with
all the available marketing

01:09:42.452 --> 01:09:45.840
information about what they
like and what they don't.

01:09:45.841 --> 01:09:48.111
They could be told,
well you're sweaty now,

01:09:48.112 --> 01:09:50.040
maybe you should get
a bottle of water.

01:09:50.041 --> 01:09:52.393
But we know you like that
store around the corner, but

01:09:52.394 --> 01:09:54.185
their offering a special
deal and so on.

01:09:54.186 --> 01:09:59.107
In other words, what I'm trying
to get at is the core conception

01:09:59.108 --> 01:10:04.136
which has some spurious basis in
certain stories about big data.

01:10:04.137 --> 01:10:08.791
To be more explicit, this is not
a good science, it's a certain

01:10:08.792 --> 01:10:13.276
story that Anderson has told,
and some very, very practical

01:10:13.277 --> 01:10:17.588
fixes in the marketing world,
which are working to install

01:10:17.589 --> 01:10:21.489
the possibility of n equals
all as a default setting.

01:10:21.490 --> 01:10:24.884
McKinseys have written reports
arguing that we need that

01:10:24.885 --> 01:10:28.348
default setting of assuming
the data would be collected,

01:10:28.349 --> 01:10:30.268
that I think raises questions.

01:10:30.269 --> 01:10:33.222
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] I heard
you say something,

01:10:33.223 --> 01:10:35.998
please go ahead,
please go ahead.

01:10:35.999 --> 01:10:38.050
&gt;&gt; It's just, I'm glad
you mentioned marketing.

01:10:38.051 --> 01:10:39.792
So in the spaces that I study,

01:10:39.793 --> 01:10:43.509
where this proposition of data
collection is always present.

01:10:43.510 --> 01:10:46.340
It's very much embedded in
marketing and advertising.

01:10:46.341 --> 01:10:47.031
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

01:10:47.032 --> 01:10:49.116
&gt;&gt; But it often still
feels as if, well,

01:10:49.117 --> 01:10:51.150
there's two things
that I think of.

01:10:51.151 --> 01:10:54.474
There's this fabulous
stuff on peak ads, right,

01:10:54.475 --> 01:10:58.492
that we sort of have already hit
the moment where the promise of

01:10:58.493 --> 01:11:00.828
advertising and
marketing is not.

01:11:00.829 --> 01:11:03.368
There's a rhetoric of it,
a mythos of it but

01:11:03.369 --> 01:11:05.560
it's not where
things really are.

01:11:05.561 --> 01:11:09.868
And so the big data stuff is
participating in a rhetoric that

01:11:09.869 --> 01:11:12.119
really needs to be revisited.

01:11:12.120 --> 01:11:16.478
So I'm glad you mentioned the
marketing cuz I do wonder how we

01:11:16.479 --> 01:11:19.897
make sense of these tensions
that sort of drag to

01:11:19.898 --> 01:11:23.071
revive a marketing and
advertising model.

01:11:23.072 --> 01:11:26.408
That actually has a lot of
empirical fragility to it,

01:11:26.409 --> 01:11:29.590
that many people actually
in the space know?

01:11:29.591 --> 01:11:30.106
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; And

01:11:30.107 --> 01:11:31.865
are a bit of
&gt;&gt; Well,

01:11:31.866 --> 01:11:34.583
again you'll point into
the fact there are complex

01:11:34.584 --> 01:11:37.499
cultural tensions being
worked out and that's good.

01:11:37.500 --> 01:11:40.854
And that's as we would expect if
this was a social process, but

01:11:40.855 --> 01:11:41.689
I can't deny.

01:11:41.690 --> 01:11:46.432
I've been very struck by Joseph
Turow's account of the retail

01:11:46.433 --> 01:11:47.780
marketing area.

01:11:47.781 --> 01:11:50.951
Which doesn't see the type
of resistance and

01:11:50.952 --> 01:11:53.710
drawback that you're
talking about.

01:11:53.711 --> 01:11:56.414
It may be different
in different sectors.

01:11:56.415 --> 01:11:59.009
So clearly, the picture
is still emerging and

01:11:59.010 --> 01:12:01.880
I am trying to draw a journal
narrative out of this.

01:12:01.881 --> 01:12:04.400
And say that if we were to
continue entirely in this

01:12:04.401 --> 01:12:07.648
direction, there will be these
major, hence, my alarm bell.

01:12:07.649 --> 01:12:09.434
But, I'm glad to hear that.

01:12:09.435 --> 01:12:10.920
And music might be
a special case.

01:12:10.921 --> 01:12:11.882
I don't know.
That's quite,

01:12:11.883 --> 01:12:14.485
or the game industry might
be very interesting.

01:12:14.486 --> 01:12:15.760
We should talk more about that,
yeah.

01:12:15.761 --> 01:12:20.211
&gt;&gt; The big data step is trying
to animate something people know

01:12:20.212 --> 01:12:23.157
is deeply crumbling
under their feet.

01:12:23.158 --> 01:12:24.480
And so there is hope
that it [INAUDIBLE] it.

01:12:24.481 --> 01:12:25.497
&gt;&gt; Right, got it.

01:12:25.498 --> 01:12:26.928
&gt;&gt; An increasing realization.

01:12:26.929 --> 01:12:29.955
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Well we should talk more, and

01:12:29.956 --> 01:12:30.979
I hope you are right.

01:12:30.980 --> 01:12:34.952
But if you look, I've also being
doing the study of the big

01:12:34.953 --> 01:12:38.846
discourse about big data
collection while economic from

01:12:38.847 --> 01:12:42.983
the leading spokes organisations
for global business, and

01:12:42.984 --> 01:12:45.902
then I'm making any
concessions at all and

01:12:45.903 --> 01:12:47.870
their language about this.

01:12:47.871 --> 01:12:50.612
And then their reports
have some weight and so

01:12:50.613 --> 01:12:53.868
that's another one of my
sources behind the report.

01:12:53.869 --> 01:12:56.069
But it's luckily
a complex picture,

01:12:56.070 --> 01:12:59.244
more complex than I
painted to the questions.

01:12:59.245 --> 01:13:00.329
Glenn, did you want to
come back and speak?

01:13:00.330 --> 01:13:04.355
&gt;&gt; There's an important
distinction that was coming up

01:13:04.356 --> 01:13:08.469
for describing between the way
in which data is used and

01:13:08.470 --> 01:13:10.358
what data is collected.

01:13:10.359 --> 01:13:10.967
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And

01:13:10.968 --> 01:13:15.344
both those seems to me different
threats when it comes to

01:13:15.345 --> 01:13:16.282
autonomy.

01:13:16.283 --> 01:13:16.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah
&gt;&gt; So

01:13:16.961 --> 01:13:20.342
one mode is data
is collected and

01:13:20.343 --> 01:13:25.352
simply the fact that it
gets surfaced in various

01:13:25.353 --> 01:13:30.373
ways in social context
undermines my control.

01:13:30.374 --> 01:13:34.678
Or my relationship to how I'm
perceived socially understood by

01:13:34.679 --> 01:13:38.184
other people and what I'm
able to do on my own because

01:13:38.185 --> 01:13:40.830
of how it would be
perceived by others.

01:13:40.831 --> 01:13:42.187
That's sort of one
set of issues.

01:13:42.188 --> 01:13:45.562
And that seems to raise issues
about you have to have humility

01:13:45.563 --> 01:13:48.474
because otherwise you get
coerced to share things In

01:13:48.475 --> 01:13:50.478
the ways that you
were describing.

01:13:50.479 --> 01:13:53.107
There's another set of
issues where actually it's

01:13:53.108 --> 01:13:55.616
the recommendations that
are coming back to me and

01:13:55.617 --> 01:13:58.140
my willingness to accept those.

01:13:58.141 --> 01:14:00.775
And the power of what's being
offered to me that's actually

01:14:00.776 --> 01:14:01.869
the threat to autonomy.

01:14:01.870 --> 01:14:02.820
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:14:02.821 --> 01:14:06.385
&gt;&gt; And the erosion of
the individual's real authority

01:14:06.386 --> 01:14:07.361
over her own.

01:14:07.362 --> 01:14:08.528
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:14:08.529 --> 01:14:10.668
&gt;&gt; Choices.

01:14:10.669 --> 01:14:15.344
And those seem to me actually to
be two somewhat separate things

01:14:15.345 --> 01:14:20.120
which are both threatening
different aspects of autonomy and-

01:14:20.121 --> 01:14:20.822
&gt;&gt; This is great.

01:14:20.823 --> 01:14:24.099
Autonomy is once you
start to open it out and

01:14:24.100 --> 01:14:26.020
rework with it from now.

01:14:26.021 --> 01:14:27.681
It's a very complex concept, but

01:14:27.682 --> 01:14:29.465
we need to fill it
in lots of new ways.

01:14:29.466 --> 01:14:31.900
So I wouldn't disagree
with that for a moment.

01:14:31.901 --> 01:14:35.403
I think they're actually
directly connected though

01:14:35.404 --> 01:14:40.250
because Hagel's view and this
is why I find it so compelling.

01:14:40.251 --> 01:14:44.166
Is that there never was a final
stability of the self it's

01:14:44.167 --> 01:14:47.927
endlessly changing in mutual
relations with others.

01:14:47.928 --> 01:14:52.309
While still trying to go
on making sense of itself.

01:14:52.310 --> 01:14:53.101
So as you say,

01:14:53.102 --> 01:14:56.534
the recommendations we receive
from others that we integrate

01:14:56.535 --> 01:14:59.647
into our sense of ourselves
are really crucial to that.

01:14:59.648 --> 01:15:01.747
Now I've been reading today and

01:15:01.748 --> 01:15:05.171
discussing in another
reading group over in

01:15:05.172 --> 01:15:08.691
tomorrow is the Karen Young
piece on the Hyper Nudge.

01:15:08.692 --> 01:15:12.198
And anyway it's quite a new
piece by legal theorists at

01:15:12.199 --> 01:15:16.242
King's College London taking
the simple liberal knowledge of

01:15:16.243 --> 01:15:18.619
the nudge relatively
the innocent.

01:15:18.620 --> 01:15:22.258
Why not encourage people to
by slightly more healthy or

01:15:22.259 --> 01:15:25.829
whatever it might be by
placing things differently.

01:15:25.830 --> 01:15:28.458
So which in itself is
not in any way offensive

01:15:28.459 --> 01:15:32.410
to liberal principles,
although that has been debated.

01:15:32.411 --> 01:15:34.654
With the hyper nudge
which is installing,

01:15:34.655 --> 01:15:37.720
nudging at almost every point
in the million point space.

01:15:37.721 --> 01:15:39.806
Every point the self
comes up against,

01:15:39.807 --> 01:15:42.843
you can immediately see you
start to get the feedback that

01:15:42.844 --> 01:15:44.562
looks like I'm talking about.

01:15:44.563 --> 01:15:49.168
And her argument is that this is
in principle necessarily opaque

01:15:49.169 --> 01:15:53.432
and therefore, it must interfere
that autonomy we thought

01:15:53.433 --> 01:15:57.420
the subject had to frame
its choices about choices.

01:15:57.421 --> 01:15:59.036
It's second all to choices.

01:15:59.037 --> 01:16:02.990
They are the core, and
that is really interesting.

01:16:02.991 --> 01:16:04.766
So I think the two
link together.

01:16:04.767 --> 01:16:08.612
And that is why I just wanna
raise the question as to whether

01:16:08.613 --> 01:16:12.931
continuous data collection as a
default on norm doesn't have and

01:16:12.932 --> 01:16:16.856
the playbacks don't necessarily
come from that when we all

01:16:16.857 --> 01:16:19.700
surveil each other as,
of course, we do.

01:16:19.701 --> 01:16:23.366
I do as much as anyone, creates
externalities moral, ethical,

01:16:23.367 --> 01:16:26.300
practical and also in terms
of social legitimacy for

01:16:26.301 --> 01:16:28.568
the institutions we need for
markets and

01:16:28.569 --> 01:16:31.256
other things which we
just have to think about.

01:16:31.257 --> 01:16:33.251
So that’s, I raise the question.

01:16:33.252 --> 01:16:37.317
&gt;&gt; Okay [INAUDIBLE] thank you.

01:16:37.318 --> 01:16:40.380
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

